## What uniform core coverage does and does not contradict

PROVED. Near-uniform r_D outside zero is compatible with genuine Boolean half-sets in general. For Paley, take D to be the nonzero quadratic residues. Then s=(q-1)/2, t=sqrt(q)/2, L=sqrt(q), h=L*1_D+t*delta_0, and for xi!=0,

    r_D(xi)=(q-3-2*chi_quadratic(xi))/4,
    L^2*r_D(xi)+2tL*1_D(xi)=C0=q(q-1)/4.

Thus r_D=(1+O(1/q))*s^2/q uniformly off zero, and the exact non-core contribution fills the deficit on D; off D the core-core term already equals C0. A claim that uniform coverage alone contradicts Booleanity is false. This example has t~sqrt(q), so it does not establish realizability in the small-t regime.

PROVED scale bookkeeping in the small-t regime. If a nearly constant core carries a fraction theta of V~q^2/4 and has size s, then its mean off-zero contribution is approximately theta*q*s/4. Nonnegativity of the bulk forces s at most approximately 4t^2/theta. This is compatible with s^2/q of logarithmic size when t=q^(1/4) times logarithms. Integer/parity fluctuations of order one then have small relative size; they do not by themselves yield a power gain. No Boolean realization with small t is asserted. The exact realization problem is the nonnegative completion u satisfying 2p*u+u*u=C0-p*p at EVERY off-zero displacement, with the fixed zero equation and trace; replacing this by independent residual variables would lose the shared translates of u.

